{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000ca1b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0aa6f-a3a8-7c72-bad2-76397b67d24d"},"sequence":872,"recorded_at":1780531400001739,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"b282fa7c-2a0d-4bf0-891b-945cd9ab9d24","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"b282fa7c-2a0d-4bf0-891b-945cd9ab9d24","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"b282fa7c-2a0d-4bf0-891b-945cd9ab9d24"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0aa73-5c2a-7252-89ce-07319ac01f32"},"task_id":"01a0aa73-5c2a-7252-89ce-07319ac01f32","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0aa73-5c2a-7252-89ce-07319ac01f32","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-36-1\",\n  \"command\": \"task-board resource add --help 2>&1 | head -n 30\\necho \\\"===LOGBOOK state===\\\"\\ngit status --short LOGBOOK.md 2>&1; echo \\\"---stash contains?---\\\"\\ngit stash show --name-only stash@{0} 2>&1 | head -n 20\\necho \\\"---LOGBOOK tail---\\\"\\ntail -n 15 LOGBOOK.md 2>&1\",\n  \"description\": \"Check resource syntax and logbook state\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"Attach a new file resource to a board element.\\n\\nSOURCE may be:\\n  - a local filesystem path\\n  - a file:// URL\\n  - an http:// or https:// URL\\n\\nExamples:\\n  task-board resource add TASK-260311-abc ./screenshot.png --type precondition\\n  task-board resource add TASK-260311-abc file:///tmp/report.pdf --name ui-report.pdf\\n  task-board resource add TASK-260311-abc ./report.md --name reports/review/report.md\\n  task-board resource add TASK-260311-abc https://example.com/spec.pdf --type outcome\\n\\nUsage:\\n  task-board resource add <ELEMENT-ID> <SOURCE> [flags]\\n\\nFlags:\\n  -d, --description string   Optional one-line description\\n  -h, --help                 help for add\\n      --name string          Override stored resource filename\\n      --type string          Resource type: precondition or outcome (default \\\"outcome\\\")\\n\\nGlobal Flags:\\n      --board-dir string         Path to the board directory (overrides TASK_BOARD_DIR and config local.board_dir) (default \\\".task-board\\\")\\n      --context-profile string   Agent context profile override: full or lite\\n      --insecure                 Skip TLS certificate verification (for self-signed certs)\\n      --json                     Output in JSON format\\n      --no-update-check          Disable automatic update check\\n      --remote string            Remote board server URL (overrides TASK_BOARD_REMOTE)\\n      --remote-board string      Remote board ID (overrides TASK_BOARD_ID)\\n===LOGBOOK state===\\n---stash contains?---\\nLOGBOOK.md\\n---LOGBOOK tail---\\n- REWORK VERIFICATION: `make m1-runtime-harness-test` passes six focused tests and all seven manifest rows. Two additional unchanged-binary manifest runs also pass 7/7 with every row privacy-safe and exact exits `0/70/71/72/73/74/74`. The complete harness filter passes 38 tests in three suites; the full SwiftPM run passes 514 tests in 44 suites with the existing 25 known unavailable-ReluxNIOSSH issues. Strict task-scoped Swift format, Core boundaries, fixture build, and diff checks exit `0`. One invalid Go-style `swift test ... -count=1` invocation exited `64` before tests and was immediately replaced by the valid Swift invocation; it is retained as command-error evidence rather than reported as a test failure.\\n\\n### 1111 — M1 integration remains isolated from the legacy SwiftPM release lane (TASK-260715-3qqbbm)\\n- BOUNDARY: The accepted `ReluxProxy` v0.1.0 SwiftPM package, `works.relux.proxy` defaults domain, product paths, and `ReluxProxy.dmg` release entries remain owned by the separate legacy repository. Generated M1 products use `ReluxProxyMac.app`, `works.relux.tunnel.mac`, `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel`, and Keychain service `works.relux.tunnel.credential.v1`; they do not define or link an exact legacy product identity.\\n- GATE: `scripts/check-migration-isolation.py`, called by the credential-free validation path, now inventories both lanes and fails closed on unreadable required inputs, cross-repository target links, identifier/defaults/Keychain collisions, generated legacy artifact names, or release-script substitution. The command can also validate the generated PBX project and both Debug/Release product trees.\\n- NEGATIVE EVIDENCE: The CLI-level mutation suite attacks the production gate with generated and legacy cross-links, bundle/defaults/Keychain/build-product collisions, and an active workflow substitution of `ReluxProxy.dmg`; every case must fail for its named reason. Narrowing only the `ReluxProxy.dmg` release marker made that named test fail while the other six rows remained green; restoring the marker returned the suite to green.\\n- OWNERSHIP: User/coexistence/import or retirement behavior remains M4 `TASK-260715-35nc5m`; release identity, entitlement, and data migration remain M5 `TASK-260715-1tzaed`. No migration is implemented by M1.\\n- BOARD ANOMALY: Pre-handoff `task-board validate` returned process exit `0` while reporting the owning Story stored as `backlog` versus child aggregate `development`. The task checklist and outcomes are complete; parent normalization remains orchestrator-owned and the validation output is retained without calling it clean.\\n\\n### 1215 — M1 runtime ownership and live-entry boundary documented (TASK-260715-2rcvr0)\\n- DOCUMENTATION: The developer/operator guide now maps provider, coordinator, factory, packet-plane, consumer, settings, snapshot, and host-projection ownership; exact startup/reverse-cleanup sequences; v1 command and compatibility bounds; M1 state/capability semantics; three distinct error surfaces; privacy-safe diagnostics; troubleshooting; reproduction commands; migration isolation; and concrete M2–M5 handoffs.\\n- ANOMALY: The implemented shared runtime and M0-bound macOS production factory are exercised by Core tests and the deterministic harness, but the checked-in generated macOS `PacketTunnelProvider` remains a no-op/version-reply shell and does not instantiate `MacOSProviderCompositionRoot`. The iOS production target is also deferred. Generated provider build success is therefore not reported as live runtime-composition evidence.\\n- SCOPE: This task makes documentation and diagram changes only. It does not wire a provider, change lifecycle behavior, claim full UDP from TCP plus safe DNS, or define migration and future-mode policy.\\n- VERIFICATION: Eight focused Swift suites pass 105 tests across messages, coordinator, provider adapter, host retirement/projection, composition gates, exact macOS graph ownership, aggregate diagnostics, and SSH redaction. The manifest-driven harness passes six tests and all seven scenario rows; both SwiftPM platform adapter targets and unsigned generated macOS host/provider Debug+Release validation pass. PlantUML source checking, four-page SVG rendering and visual inspection, 52 local-link checks, source-contract checks, and `git diff --check` pass.\\n- REVIEW REWORK: CR revision 1 correctly identified that the partial-failure diagram placed packet activation after failed settings applies with `committed` or `uncertain` disposition. The corrected page separates `notCommitted` failure (no clear), `committed`/`uncertain` failure (clear, no activation), and successful committed apply followed by activation failure (clear). The production coordinator and its 22-test negative lifecycle suite verify this boundary.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 6279,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 1570,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","final_result":true}}}
