required inputs, cross-repository target links, identifier/defaults/Keychain collisions, generated legacy artifact names, or release-script substitution. The command can also validate the generated PBX project and both Debug/Release product trees.\\n- NEGATIVE EVIDENCE: The CLI-level mutation suite attacks the production gate with generated and legacy cross-links, bundle/defaults/Keychain/build-product collisions, and an active workflow substitution of `ReluxProxy.dmg`; every case must fail for its named reason. Narrowing only the `ReluxProxy.dmg` release marker made that named test fail while the other six rows remained green; restoring the marker returned the suite to green.\\n- OWNERSHIP: User/coexistence/import or retirement behavior remains M4 `TASK-260715-35nc5m`; release identity, entitlement, and data migration remain M5 `TASK-260715-1tzaed`. No migration is implemented by M1.\\n- BOARD ANOMALY: Pre-handoff `task-board validate` returned process exit `0` while reporting the owning Story stored as `backlog` versus child aggregate `development`. The task checklist and outcomes are complete; parent normalization remains orchestrator-owned and the validation output is retained without calling it clean.\\n\\n### 1215 — M1 runtime ownership and live-entry boundary documented (TASK-260715-2rcvr0)\\n- DOCUMENTATION: The developer/operator guide now maps provider, coordinator, factory, packet-plane, consumer, settings, snapshot, and host-projection ownership; exact startup/reverse-cleanup sequences; v1 command and compatibility bounds; M1 state/capability semantics; three distinct error surfaces; privacy-safe diagnostics; troubleshooting; reproduction commands; migration isolation; and concrete M2–M5 handoffs.\\n- ANOMALY: The implemented shared runtime and M0-bound macOS production factory are exercised by Core tests and the deterministic harness, but the checked-in generated macOS `PacketTunnelProvider` remains a no-op/version-reply shell and does not instantiate `